her to answer, a dancer and a crowd on the beat, grades, the audio offset | songs, rhythm | #rhythm, #rhythm/stepup, #rhythm/rapschool, #rhythm/calibrate |\\n| RPG | [rpg.js](rpg.js), [rpg.html](rpg.html) | a JRPG's journey: a town, a world map, a cave and its boss, the ATB battle, menus and save slots | story, rpg | #rpg, #rpg/battle |\\n| ROGUELIKE | [roguelike.js](roguelike.js), [roguelike.html](roguelike.html) | a turn-based crypt of ten generated floors: fields of view and fog, turns by speed, potions and scrolls; Rogue, Mystery Dungeon | grid, gen | #roguelike |\\n| TACTICS | [tactics.js](tactics.js), [tactics.html](tactics.html) | an isometric battle on a map of heights: charge time or phases, facing, arcs, areas, a scoring AI | grid, story | #tactics, #tactics/battle, #tactics/phases |\\n| RTS | [rts.js](rts.js), [rts.html](rts.html) | real-time strategy: workers, gold, a barracks and a stable, three kinds to fight, fog, an enemy camp that builds and attacks in waves | grid, strategy | #rts |\\n| SIM | [sim.js](sim.js), [sim.html](sim.html) | a theme park: paths, four rides and two shops, guests with needs and money, prices, a rating, day and night, time controls | grid, strategy | #sim |\\n| BRAWLER | [brawler.js](brawler.js), [brawler.html](brawler.html) | a beat-'em-up: a city block at dusk, combos, grabs and kicks, waves that lock the screen, a second player who drops in; Final Fight, Streets of Rage | none | #brawler |\\n| FIGHTING | [fighting.js](fighting.js), [fighting.html](fighting.html) | one on one: three fighters, versus, arcade, training | fight | #fighting, #fighting/versus, #fighting/arcade, #fighting/training |\\n| ANIMATION LAB | [animlab.js](animlab.js), [animlab.html](animlab.html) | every move, pose, skin and view of the character rigs | none | #animlab |\\n| KART | [kart.js](kart.js), [kart.html](kart.html) | Mode 7 karts: six on a circuit, three laps, items, two players in a split screen | mode7, drive | #kart, #kart/race, #kart/vs |\\n| ROAD | [road.js](road.js), [road.html](road.html) | a pseudo-3D coast road against the clock: curves, hills, a tunnel, traffic, checkpoints, a radio | road | #road, #road/drive |\\n| SPORTS | [sports.js](sports.js), [sports.html](sports.html) | two against two in a walled cage, up to four players: a ball that bounces and curls, passes, lobs, shots on a meter, formations, a match clock; Sensible Soccer and NBA Jam | none | #sports, #sports/match, #sports/vs |\\n| STEALTH | [stealth.js](stealth.js), [stealth.html](stealth.html) | third-person stealth and parkour: a rooftop district at dusk, guards with sight and suspicion, climbing, takedowns, an HTML HUD; Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear | none | #stealth |\\n\\n**3D:** the 3D engine (three.js and Rapier) and its kits, the playbook's 3D rows, are not in this package. They are on the\\nsite (my-3d2dge.vercel.app/3d, /kits/3d-<genre>) and in the repository (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge:\\n`engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`, its header the 3D manual; `src/starter3d/`). Here, a 3D game is built on the 2D engine's views,\\na world in x, y and z drawn from any angle (`API.md`, Coordinates).\\n======\\nAI_GUIDE.md:44:## Visual craft: aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES\\nAI_GUIDE.md:500:**Real-time strategy (Dune II, Warcraft II, Command & Conquer, StarCraft).** Start from the RTS slice (the grid and strategy modules). The strategy module (the full engine, after the grid module) keeps the rules; the game draws.\\nAI_GUIDE.md:538:- Lemmings: an `E.Terrain` level (a picture's cells as materials), each lemming a walker (`land.walk`: a step up of 5, a fall that kills past a landing speed), diggers and bashers carving a small circle or a polygon each step, builders adding a short bar (`addPoly`); hundreds of walkers are cheap (no physics bodies).\\nAI_GUIDE.md:581:- A bomb is a cell with a fuse (seconds in your state); its blast runs out cell by cell in four lines (`grid.line`) up to its range, stopping at a hard block, breaking the first soft one and lighting other bombs in its way (a chain). Power-ups drop from broken blocks: range, bombs, speed, a kick (`mover.update(..., { push })`).\",\"stderr\":\"\\nShell cwd was reset to /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174740__x7ef5ea/work\",\"interrupted\":false,\"isImage\":false,\"noOutputExpected\":false},\"tool_result_meta\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_01K1UFscZXvnEsJANEsnYecS\",\"permission_decision\":{\"decision\":\"accept\",\"source\":\"config\",\"reason_type\":\"mode\"}}]}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"task_updated\",\"task_id\":\"baxlppxas\",\"run_id\":\"0mw1ef8rd-132ffdff\",\"patch\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"end_time\":1793753576295},\"uuid\":\"ea0a2ef8-95f0-463a-a674-ecbc4cff9857\",\"session_id\":\"5176bdbd-8f33-407b-b89e-9fc1c745933d\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"task_notification\",\"task_id\":\"baxlppxas\",\"run_id\":\"0mw1ef8rd-132ffdff\",\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01PaG5yethRLWHnbM9tWapAk\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"output_file\":\"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-max-plan--20261103-174740--x7ef5ea-work/5176bdbd-8f33-407b-b89e-9fc1c745933d/tasks/baxlppxas.output\",\"summary\":\"Background command \\\"Clone engine and AI Hub repos into /var/tmp scratch\\\" completed (exit code 0)\",\"uuid\":\"df03b975-23d8-4983-8832-bf11429b0adf\",\"session_id\":\"5176bdbd-8f33-407b-b89e-9fc1c745933d\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"background_tasks_changed\",\"tasks\":[],\"uuid\":\"6fb003cf-ec4f-4c90-b8f1-72250dd9b3ed\",\"session_id\":\"5176bdbd-8f33-407b-b89e-9fc1c745933d\"}"]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174746__xe37f55/agent.out","hunks":[{"oldStart":200,"oldLines":3,"newStart":200,"newLines":5,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011CggyR2aSW61GshoTwapC4\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01MEF83YGcJwQSw3Y8MZ8c9f\",\"name\":\"Bash\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"cd /home/micha/.cache/opwolf-plan-research-OCq3d6/hub && sed -n 670,720p README.md\",\"description\":\"Read the AI Hub README section on game metadata tags\"},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":919,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":116743,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":919},\"output_tokens\":20,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"global\",\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"d128962a-bd2b-4da0-bea3-10df26a8e825\",\"uuid\":\"7cb90f4e-cee0-459c-8e1e-882350caaf1e\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T00:52:53.326Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011CggyR2Mo4B5VAY41nYuYe\",\"wire_tool_inputs\":{\"toolu_01MEF83YGcJwQSw3Y8MZ8c9f\":{\"command\":\"cd /home/micha/.cache/opwolf-plan-research-OCq3d6/hub && sed -n 670,720p README.md\",\"description\":\"Read the AI Hub README section on game metadata tags\"}}}"," {\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01MEF83YGcJwQSw3Y8MZ8c9f\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"    \\\"model\\\": \\\"Claude Opus 5\\\",\\n    \\\"harness\\\": \\\"Claude Code\\\",\\n    \\\"status\\\": \\\"polished\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"pixel-art\\\", \\\"dungeon\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nHere is the part worth knowing: **the filter rails are not declared anywhere.**\\nThey are read off whatever the games actually carry. Fill in `model` on a few\\ngames and a Model rail appears on the wall by itself; remove them and it goes.\\nAny field works, including ones invented on the spot. A game can also carry its\\nown, with `<meta name=\\\"hub:model\\\" content=\\\"…\\\">` in its head. A field whose\\nvalues are nearly all different (a build id, a URL) is searchable but never\\nbecomes a rail. Every field is searchable. `harness` is searched and shown in\\nthe drawer but never gets a rail, and neither do `source` and `series`, which\\nhave Original, Inspired and the stacks.\\n\\n`collection` comes from the folder a game sits in, but it is an ordinary field:\\nan override, or a `hub:collection` meta, replaces it.\\n\\nThe library's own catalogue uses these fields, all in `data/overrides.json`:\\n\\n| Field | What it is | On the wall |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `genre` | one of a fixed list (Racing, Platformer, Life Sim, Tech Demo…) | the Genre rail, and the cabinet's panel |\\n| `type` | `Game`, `Tech demo` or `Tool` | the shelf: games, or tech demos & tools |\\n| `tags` | 2–5 from a fixed vocabulary (`3d`, `retro`, `co-op`, `touch`…) | the Tags rail |\\n| `players` | `1–2`, `1–4`… only when a game has co-op or versus | the Players rail: *Single player*, or *Multiplayer*, which drops a menu of head counts (*4 players* finds every game that seats four). A game with no `players` is single player |\\n| `source` | the classic a game riffs on, as it is titled (`Dragon Quest IX`); none for an original | Original or Inspired, the stacks, the drawer, and search |\\n| `series` | the franchise, only when it is not the source itself (`Dragon Quest` for `Dragon Quest IX`) | the stacks |\\n| `controls` | a one-line summary | the drawer |\\n| `description` | one or two plain sentences | the drawer |\\n\\nEvery game with the same `source` must have the same `series`; `pnpm test`\\nchecks. A game can name its own source with `<meta name=\\\"hub:source\\\">`\\n(`hub:inspired-by`, the older name, still works). Source leads the rail strip.\\nGenre, Collection, Type and Players follow it when they exist; the\\nrest follow by how many games carry them. `docs/curation-2026-09-24.md`\\nrecords how the library was curated: which file of each game was kept, and\\nwhich older iterations it replaced. `docs/curation-2026-09-28.md` records the\\nimport from the source-named GPT-games folder and maps the old slugs to the\\ncurrent ones; `docs/curation-2026-09-28-new-ai-stuff.md` records the smaller\\nimport that followed it.\\n\\nSetting `\\\"hidden\\\": true` drops a game from the catalogue entirely.\\n`\\\"restricted\\\": true` keeps it in the catalogue but off the wall, where only dev\\nsettings can bring it back — used for the companionship-business sims.\\n\\n### Dev settings\\nShell cwd was reset to /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174746__xe37f55/work\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"d128962a-bd2b-4da0-bea3-10df26a8e825\",\"uuid\":\"cbe64715-9c12-491d-9385-95065e39129b\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T00:52:53.341Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"stdout\":\"    \\\"model\\\": \\\"Claude Opus 5\\\",\\n    \\\"harness\\\": \\\"Claude Code\\\",\\n    \\\"status\\\": \\\"polished\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"pixel-art\\\", \\\"dungeon\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nHere is the part worth knowing: **the filter rails are not declared anywhere.**\\nThey are read off whatever the games actually carry. Fill in `model` on a few\\ngames and a Model rail appears on the wall by itself; remove them and it goes.\\nAny field works, including ones invented on the spot. A game can also carry its\\nown, with `<meta name=\\\"hub:model\\\" content=\\\"…\\\">` in its head. A field whose\\nvalues are nearly all different (a build id, a URL) is searchable but never\\nbecomes a rail. Every field is searchable. `harness` is searched and shown in\\nthe drawer but never gets a rail, and neither do `source` and `series`, which\\nhave Original, Inspired and the stacks.\\n\\n`collection` comes from the folder a game sits in, but it is an ordinary field:\\nan override, or a `hub:collection` meta, replaces it.\\n\\nThe library's own catalogue uses these fields, all in `data/overrides.json`:\\n\\n| Field | What it is | On the wall |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `genre` | one of a fixed list (Racing, Platfo