layStation 2 (July 21, 2005, as *Karate Dou [Oretachi Geasen Zoku]*), PS4 PSN (October 9, 2014, *Arcade Archives - Karate Dou*), and PS4 PSN (July 2, 2015, *Arcade Archives - Karate Dou*, listed as an [AS] entry).\\\"}]},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"6ff8a465-a3dc-4463-ad61-3d7e016e47ef\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"c3d9b652-18ad-4619-9129-9c071139f5a6\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-04T00:56:56.294Z\\\",\\\"tool_use_result\\\":{\\\"bytes\\\":47626,\\\"code\\\":200,\\\"codeText\\\":\\\"OK\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"The page doesn't contain most of the items you listed. Here's what it does include:\\\\n\\\\n**Controls:** The page doesn't chart specific stick-to-technique mappings. It says the game used \\\\\\\"Double 4-way joysticks\\\\\\\" and that \\\\\\\"simultaneous joystick manipulation required to execute even the simplest of kicks.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n**Bonus stages:** \\\\\\\"After every second victory, the player has a chance to earn bonus points by dodging or striking targets.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n**Not on the page:** judge trivia, speech lines, the girl, stage names, ranks, and designer/staff credits.\\\\n\\\\n**Release info:**\\\\n- \\\\\\\"Karate Dou was released in September 1984 in Japan.\\\\\\\"\\\\n- \\\\\\\"This game is known outside Japan as Karate Champ.\\\\\\\"\\\\n- Copyright: \\\\\\\"Karate Dou © 1984 Data East.\\\\\\\"\\\\n- Ports: Famicom Disk (July 22, 1988, as Karate Champ), PlayStation 2 (July 21, 2005, as *Karate Dou [Oretachi Geasen Zoku]*), PS4 PSN (October 9, 2014, *Arcade Archives - Karate Dou*), and PS4 PSN (July 2, 2015, *Arcade Archives - Karate Dou*, listed as an [AS] entry).\\\",\\\"durationMs\\\":5950,\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://www.arcade-history.com/game/1261/\\\"},\\\"tool_result_meta\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"toolu_0191BRmj1G8ooN46M9H632gD\\\",\\\"permission_decision\\\":{\\\"decision\\\":\\\"accept\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"config\\\",\\\"reason_type\\\":\\\"mode\\\"}}]}\"]}]},{\"filePath\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claud