{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VBWFJXs9of4hbqc5kv1Tbw","type":"tool_result","content":"898\t\n899\t### 8.5 Play suite (the scripted playthrough of the whole game)\n900\t\n901\t1. **Full descent, deterministic:** a fixed seed, two bot heroes (Berserker and Hexer) with God mode on and no other cheats, from the title through class select, floors 1–8, both treasure rooms, the boss, the ending, the credits and the first floor of Descent II. Asserts the floor order, that every floor is finished by the bot within a time limit (6 minutes of game time each), the boss's phases in order, the ending scene and the score table. Run twice: the same `game.stateHash` both times.\n902\t2. **Balance smoke (no cheats):** one bot hero on Easy, floors 1–3: it reaches floor 4 alive. One bot on Normal, floor 1: finishes with 300+ health. Metrics (time, health at the stairs, food eaten, kills) are written to `tests/out/balance.json` for tuning.\n903\t3. **Attract loop:** start the attract mode headless, run 90 s: every segment plays, no errors, it loops; an input exits.\n904\t4. **Local four-player:** four scripted inputs (two keyboard halves via `addPlayer({ keys })`, two pads via `addPlayer({ pad })`), joins mid-floor, a drop-out, the leash.\n905\t\n906\t### 8.6 Net suite (Node) and hub suite (browser)\n907\t\n908\t- **Fake hub (Node):** N game copies, each in its own vm context, stepped together on one virtual clock; a fake `AIHub` per copy with `connect`, `ready`, `on`, `send` (`to`, `reliable`), `time`, `finish`, `rng`; a simulated network (latency, jitter, loss, reliable ordering). Checks: host + 2 clients and a server scenario; clients see the host's monsters move; a client's pickup is confirmed (and a race between two clients gives the item to one); doors, kills and narrator lines arrive everywhere; a client's hero takes damage from host monsters; magic from a client works; **no copy sends more than 20 messages in any second**; every message under 16 KB, snapshots under 4 KB in a 100-monster scene; time-up → one `finish` with valid standings within 5 s; leave and host-leave handled; `end` stops input, sends and sound.\n909\t- **The real harness (browser, Playwright):** copy `hub/harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` and the built game into `<dev>/.serve/`; start a static server bound to `127.0.0.1` on port 0 (Node's `http` module listening on port 0, or `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`); fetch the game's URL and confirm its `<title>` is Gauntletdown; open in turn:\n910\t  1. `harness.html?game=<file>&players=2&auto=1`\n911\t  2. `harness.html?game=<file>&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`\n912\t  3. the same with `&scenario=host-leave`\n913\t  4. `harness.html?game=<file>&players=2&scenario=server&auto=1`\n914\t\n915\t  Wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` and require `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`; on failure print each failed check's name and detail. Stop the server by its PID. Note: the harness presses arrow keys and Enter into the frames (its letter keys arrive with `code: 'a'`, which the engine ignores), which is enough for traffic and the Ready check.\n916\t\n917\t### 8.7 UI suite (browser) and screenshots\n918\t\n919\t- **Built file from `file://`:** no errors, no network requests; the title draws (non-blank canvas); 10.5 s idle → `attract`; a key press → `title`; a mouse hover moves the focus on the class cards and a click picks.\n920\t- **Touch-only emulation** (`hasTouch`, `isMobile`, no fine pointer): the notice shows (`snapshot().touchOnly`), the corner menu button exists, no touch buttons exist, the attract mode starts after 10 s.\n921\t- **Screenshots** (for eyes, not assertions) at 1920×1080 and 1280×720 into `<dev>/shots/`: title, class select, How to Play, floors 1, 4 and 7, four-player HUD, a magic blast, the boss, pause, Settings, Dev Tools, About. Check readability from \"10 feet\" (the image at a quarter size), the look notes from `node tools/check.mjs`, and filmstrips (`tools/filmstrip.mjs --seed 1`) of every class's throw and melee and every monster's attack telegraph.\n922\t\n923\t---\n924\t\n925\t## 9. Done checklist (every TASK.md requirement, and where it is met)\n926\t\n927\t| TASK.md asks for | Met by |\n928\t|---|---|\n929\t| Genre and perspective that fit the source | Arcade co-op dungeon crawler, three-quarter overhead (1, 4.13) |\n930\t| Title: a riff, 50 brainstormed, picked with a real RNG | Gauntletdown (top of this plan) |\n931\t| Single HTML named per TASK.md in the root; dev files in the subfolder | 2, 3.6 |\n932\t| Built on my-3D2dge `dist/handoff`, engine changes allowed | 3.1, 3.5 |\n933\t| Engine's native style plus new art for the source's feel | 4.22 |\n934\t| Engine's native animation plus new animations | 4.23 |\n935\t| All music, sound and voices in code; gibberish voices | 4.21 |\n936\t| AI Hub integration, and the game on its own | 4.20, 8.6 |\n937\t| Feature-complete demo showing all mechanics; polished vertical slice; infrastructure for more | 4, 5, registries (3.2), `docs/DESIGNER.md` |\n938\t| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset to defaults | 4.17 |\n939\t| Attract mode after 10 s idle on title/menus; any input exits; may cheat | 4.18, 8.4, 8.7 |\n940\t| Narrative tone like the source's | 4.12, 5.1, 5.6 |\n941\t| 10-foot controller-first UI; native keyboard and mouse; prompts follow the last device; PlayStation glyphs, Xbox option | 4.3, 4.14, 4.15, E5, E6 |\n942\t| No dense web-style data screens | 4.14, 4.15, 5.8 |\n943\t| Touch-only: no touch controls, the notice, the settings button, attract runs | 4.19, 8.7 |\n944\t| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | 6 (cut list removes whole features only) |\n945\t| Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback in the chat reply and an About/Credits screen | 5.8, Appendix B |\n946\t| `exit-interview.json` in the exact shape, parsing as JSON | Appendix A (check with `node -e \"JSON.parse(require('fs').readFileSync('exit-interview.json','utf8'))\"`) |\n947\t| Headless testing, a scripted playthrough, one command printing only failures and a count | 8 |\n948\t| Server on 127.0.0.1, port 0, confirm the page, stop only your own PIDs | 8.6 |\n949\t| No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit | 0 |\n950\t\n951\t---\n952\t\n953\t## Appendix A: `exit-interview.json` prefill\n954\t\n955\tWrite it last, in this folder's root beside the .html, in exactly this shape. Fill every value; \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm. Plain text only. The `aiHub` block below is ready to use.\n956\t\n957\t```json\n958\t{\n959\t  \"sourceInformation\": {\n960\t    \"date\": \"2026-11-03 17:48:04 (your TASK.md's date)\",\n961\t    \"source\": \"Gauntlet (Atari Games, 1985 arcade)\",\n962\t    \"plan\": \"PLAN.md\",\n963\t    \"genre\": \"Arcade co-op dungeon crawler (top-down hack-and-shoot)\",\n964\t    \"perspective\": \"Three-quarter overhead (top-down and isometric selectable)\",\n965\t    \"tech\": \"my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (patched), HTML5 Canvas 2D, Web Audio, optional WebGPU lighting, AI Hub session client 1.4.0; tests on Node and Playwright 1.63.0\",\n966\t    \"artDirection\": \"...\",\n967\t    \"animation\": \"...\",\n968\t    \"audio\": \"...\",\n969\t    \"online\": \"AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 1-4 players; local co-op 1-4 outside sessions\",\n970\t    \"testing\": \"...\",\n971\t    \"research\": \"...\",\n972\t    \"environment\": \"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\",\n973\t    \"model\": \"(your model and reasoning effort)\",\n974\t    \"usage\": \"(estimates if available, else unknown)\"\n975\t  },\n976\t  \"testingFeedback\": [\"How many issues did testing catch?\", \"How many were critical?\", \"Most helpful test\", \"Least helpful test\"],\n977\t  \"engineFeedback\": [\"Liked most\", \"Stuck or guessed wrong?\", \"Most important thing built\", \"Hardest part\", \"What changed in the engine and why (see ENGINE-CHANGES.md)\", \"One thing to change\"],\n978\t  \"promptFeedback\": [\"Liked most\", \"Confusing\", \"Hardest\", \"Would change\"],\n979\t  \"aiHub\": {\n980\t    \"name\": \"Gauntletdown\",\n981\t    \"tagline\": \"Four fools. One hungry dungeon.\",\n982\t    \"description\": \"A one-to-four player co-op dungeon crawl inspired by Gauntlet: blast through hordes, smash monster generators and eat before your life force runs out. Play together on one screen, or online in AI Hub sessions.\",\n983\t    \"type\": \"Game\",\n984\t    \"genre\": \"Action\",\n985\t    \"source\": \"Gauntlet\",\n986\t    \"series\": \"Gauntlet\",\n987\t    \"players\": \"1–4\",\n988\t    \"controls\": \"Move WASD/left stick · Aim mouse/right stick · Fire click/R2 · Magic right-click/△ · Pause Esc/Options\",\n989\t    \"tags\": [\"top-down\", \"co-op\", \"arcade\", \"fantasy\", \"comedy\", \"retro\"]\n990\t  }\n991\t}\n992\t```\n993\t\n994\t(The arrays hold your answers, one string per question, in order; the placeholders above show which question goes where.)\n995\t\n996\t## Appendix B: Source Information draft (finish it with your own facts)\n997\t\n998\t- **Date:** from your TASK.md (here 2026-11-03 17:48:04)\n999\t- **Source:** Gauntlet (Atari Games, 1985 arcade)\n1000\t- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n1001\t- **Genre and Perspective:** arcade co-op dungeon crawler (top-down hack-and-shoot); three-quarter overhead, with top-down and isometric options\n1002\t- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (handoff, with the engine changes in ENGINE-CHANGES.md), Canvas 2D, Web Audio, optional WebGPU lighting, AI Hub session client 1.4.0; Node and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests\n1003\t- **Art Direction:** the engine's PS1-era 2D look: procedural HD rigs, textured materials, canvas lighting; three realms (Larder, Ossuary, Furnace Gut) and a banquet hall; new gear, the Shade ghost rig, generators, sprites; generic props from the engine's CC0 asset store\n1004\t- **Animation:** the engine's procedural rigs and E.MOVES, with new throws, bow draw, eating, starving gait, generator states, ghost cloth, boss choreography\n1005\t- **Audio:** nine original chip songs with dynamic layers, synthesized sound effects, gibberish (Babble) voices for the narrator, heroes, monsters and the King; no audio files\n1006\t- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared world, 1–4 players; local co-op 1–4 on its own\n1007\t- **Testing:** what you ran (the suites, counts, the harness runs)\n1008\t- **Research:** what you looked up (this plan's planner read the engine handoff docs and the AI Hub protocol and harness; Gauntlet facts from the planner's own knowledge, no web research)\n1009\t- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n1010\t- **Model:** yours\n1011\t- **Usage:** your estimates, or unknown\n1012\t\n1013\t## Appendix C: Engine and hub facts this plan relies on (verified while planning)\n1014\t\n1015\t- Engine **v0.85.2** handoff: `my-3d2dge.js` is the full engine with every module; `game/test.mjs` shows the vm loader; `kits/test.mjs` shows the stand-ins (`window`, `addEventListener`, `location`); `tools/check.mjs`, `tools/filmstrip.mjs`, `tools/replay.mjs`, `tools/asset-find.mjs`, `tools/asset-sheet.mjs`, `tools/asset-set.mjs`; `package.json` pins Playwright 1.63.0.\n1016\t- `AI_GUIDE.md` has a **Gauntlet recipe** (ADVENTURE slice, `allowJoin({ max: 4 })`, `cam.frame`, one shared FlowField, rigs with `every`) and a **Twin-stick recipe** (`input.padAim`, pointer aim, an `E.Bullets` pool).\n1017\t- `E.FlowField` has one target (`update(x, y)`, BFS over 4 neighbours, rebuilt when the target's cell or `map.version` changes): hence E1.\n1018\t- `E.Bullets` fields: `x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, r, dmg, life, team, color, core, sprite, pierce, grav, data`; wall hits compare `map.heightAt(x, y) > z` (shots and lobs fly over low walls); no bounce: hence E3.\n1019\t- Input presets bind `pause` to `Escape, KeyP, Pad9`; `PAD_GLYPHS` has `playstation` and `xbox`; the `glyphs` setting defaults to `'auto'` in the Controller group: hence E5. `input.aimSource` is `'move' | 'mouse' | 'pad'`; `input.padAim` is the right stick; `game.mouseGround()` the mouse's ground point.\n1020\t- Settings: `settings.define({ key, label, type: 'toggle'|'slider'|'select', value, min, max, step, options, group, apply })`, `settings.reset(group)`; E.Shell renders a slider row as a range input, adjustable by keys and pad. Its CSS is 14 px base: hence E6.\n1021\t- `game.menu('settings')` over a non-pausable scene makes the scene wait (it pauses the game): hence the session's own non-pausing overlay. `_autoPause` only pauses pausable scenes.\n1022\t- `E.Arcade`: `start`, `add`, `die`, `continue`, `table`, `qualifies`, `enter`, `drawTable`, `card`, `say`, `attract` (idle 20 s by default; this game runs its own 10 s attract director).\n1023\t- Humanoid weapons are `'sword' | 'gun' | 'staff' | null`, drawn in a depth-sorted list in `_drawHD`: hence E2. `E.MOVES` includes `throw`, `cast`, `bash`, `overhead`, `spin`, `thrust`, `jab`, `cross`.\n1024\t- Audio: `ChipAudio` has `_master`, `_sfxBus`, `_musBus` on `audio.ctx`: hence E8.\n1025\t- The asset store has food (`kenney-food`), dungeon props (`kaykit-dungeon-remastered`, incl. spikes and chests), crypt props (`kaykit-halloween-bits`, `kenney-graveyard`), a potion (`kenney-mini-dungeon`), a key sprite (`kenney-tiny-town:tile-0117`); torches, candelabras, chests and doors are also `r.prop` names.\n1026\t- AI Hub **Session Protocol v1**, client **1.4.0**: the client goes first in `<head>`, unmodified; metas `hub:session`, `-players`, `-mode`, `-goal`, `-howto`, optional `-minutes`; `connect({ mode, seats, card })`, `ready()` once, `answer('ready'|'more')`, `time()`, `send(data, { to, reliable })` (JSON ≤ 16 KB; ≤ 20 messages a second, bursts to 40; unreliable over about 1,200 bytes splits into packets and is lost whole if one is), `finish({ standings, scores, won, stats, awards })` by the host only, events `card`, `start`, `message`, `leave`, `time-up` (`graceMs`, 5 s in the harness), `end`. In shared world `Math.random` is not seeded; `AIHub.rng(label)` is the same on every copy. The harness: `?game=&players=&auto=1`, plus `latency`, `jitter`, `loss`, `scenario=host-leave|server`; pass is `window.__hubCheck.pass`, completion `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'`; it must be served from the same origin as the game.\n1027\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01565e32-5dd5-4107-91e6-1b53667edea6","uuid":"fa118453-bf4b-48e4-8fcd-3b34b8b8a5e4","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:30:20.204Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174804__x8cc180/work/PLAN.md","content":"\n### 8.5 Play suite (the scripted playthrough of the whole game)\n\n1. **Full descent, deterministic:** a fixed seed, two bot heroes (Berserker and Hexer) with God mode on and no other cheats, from the title through class select, floors 1–8, both treasure rooms, the boss, the ending, the credits and the first floor of Descent II. Asserts the floor order, that every floor is finished by the bot within a time limit (6 minutes of game time each), the boss's phases in order, the ending scene and the score table. Run twice: the same `game.stateHash` both times.\n2. **Balance smoke (no cheats):** one bot hero on Easy, floors 1–3: it reaches floor 4 alive. One bot on Normal, floor 1: finishes with 300+ health. Metrics (time, health at the stairs, food eaten, kills) are written to `tests/out/balance.json` for tuning.\n3. **Attract loop:** start the attract mode headless, run 90 s: every segment plays, no errors, it loops; an input exits.\n4. **Local four-player:** four scripted inputs (two keyboard halves via `addPlayer({ keys })`, two pads via `addPlayer({ pad })`), joins mid-floor, a drop-out, the leash.\n\n### 8.6 Net suite (Node) and hub suite (browser)\n\n- **Fake hub (Node):** N game copies, each in its own vm context, stepped together on one virtual clock; a fake `AIHub` per copy with `connect`, `ready`, `on`, `send` (`to`, `reliable`), `time`, `finish`, `rng`; a simulated network (latency, jitter, loss, reliable ordering). Checks: host + 2 clients and a server scenario; clients see the host's monsters move; a client's pickup is confirmed (and a race between two clients gives the item to one); doors, kills and narrator lines arrive everywhere; a client's hero takes damage from host monsters; magic from a client works; **no copy sends more than 20 messages in any second**; every message under 16 KB, snapshots under 4 KB in a 100-monster scene; time-up → one `finish` with valid standings within 5 s; leave and host-leave handled; `end` stops input, sends and sound.\n- **The real harness (browser, Playwright):** copy `hub/harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` and the built game into `<dev>/.serve/`; start a static server bound to `127.0.0.1` on port 0 (Node's `http` module listening on port 0, or `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`); fetch the game's URL and confirm its `<title>` is Gauntletdown; open in turn:\n  1. `harness.html?game=<file>&players=2&auto=1`\n  2. `harness.html?game=<file>&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`\n  3. the same with `&scenario=host-leave`\n  4. `harness.html?game=<file>&players=2&scenario=server&auto=1`\n\n  Wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` and require `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`; on failure print each failed check's name and detail. Stop the server by its PID. Note: the harness presses arrow keys and Enter into the frames (its letter keys arrive with `code: 'a'`, which the engine ignores), which is enough for traffic and the Ready check.\n\n### 8.7 UI suite (browser) and screenshots\n\n- **Built file from `file://`:** no errors, no network requests; the title draws (non-blank canvas); 10.5 s idle → `attract`; a key press → `title`; a mouse hover moves the focus on the class cards and a click picks.\n- **Touch-only emulation** (`hasTouch`, `isMobile`, no fine pointer): the notice shows (`snapshot().touchOnly`), the corner menu button exists, no touch buttons exist, the attract mode starts after 10 s.\n- **Screenshots** (for eyes, not assertions) at 1920×1080 and 1280×720 into `<dev>/shots/`: title, class select, How to Play, floors 1, 4 and 7, four-player HUD, a magic blast, the boss, pause, Settings, Dev Tools, About. Check readability from \"10 feet\" (the image at a quarter size), the look notes from `node tools/check.mjs`, and filmstrips (`tools/filmstrip.mjs --seed 1`) of every class's throw and melee and every monster's attack telegraph.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Done checklist (every TASK.md requirement, and where it is met)\n\n| TASK.md asks for | Met by |\n|---|---|\n| Genre and perspective that fit the source | Arcade co-op dungeon crawler, three-quarter overhead (1, 4.13) |\n| Title: a riff, 50 brainstormed, picked with a real RNG | Gauntletdown (top of this plan) |\n| Single HTML named per TASK.md in the root; dev files in the subfolder | 2, 3.6 |\n| Built on my-3D2dge `dist/handoff`, engine changes allowed | 3.1, 3.5 |\n| Engine's native style plus new art for the source's feel | 4.22 |\n| Engine's native animation plus new animations | 4.23 |\n| All music, sound and voices in code; gibberish voices | 4.21 |\n| AI Hub integration, and the game on its own | 4.20, 8.6 |\n| Feature-complete demo showing all mechanics; polished vertical slice; infrastructure for more | 4, 5, registries (3.2), `docs/DESIGNER.md` |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset to defaults | 4.17 |\n| Attract mode after 10 s idle on title/menus; any input exits; may cheat | 4.18, 8.4, 8.7 |\n| Narrative tone like the source's | 4.12, 5.1, 5.6 |\n| 10-foot controller-first UI; native keyboard and mouse; prompts follow the last device; PlayStation glyphs, Xbox option | 4.3, 4.14, 4.15, E5, E6 |\n| No dense web-style data screens | 4.14, 4.15, 5.8 |\n| Touch-only: no touch controls, the notice, the settings button, attract runs | 4.19, 8.7 |\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | 6 (cut list removes whole features only) |\n| Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback in the chat reply and an About/Credits screen | 5.8, Appendix B |\n| `exit-interview.json` in the exact shape, parsing as JSON | Appendix A (check with `node -e \"JSON.parse(require('fs').readFileSync('exit-interview.json','utf8'))\"`) |\n| Headless testing, a scripted playthrough, one command printing only failures and a count | 8 |\n| Server on 127.0.0.1, port 0, confirm the page, stop only your own PIDs | 8.6 |\n| No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit | 0 |\n\n---\n\n## Appendix A: `exit-interview.json` prefill\n\nWrite it last, in this folder's root beside the .html, in exactly this shape. Fill every value; \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm. Plain text only. The `aiHub` block below is ready to use.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\n    \"date\": \"2026-11-03 17:48:04 (your TASK.md's date)\",\n    \"source\": \"Gauntlet (Atari Games, 1985 arcade)\",\n    \"plan\": \"PLAN.md\",\n    \"genre\": \"Arcade co-op dungeon crawler (top-down hack-and-shoot)\",\n    \"perspective\": \"Three-quarter overhead (top-down and isometric selectable)\",\n    \"tech\": \"my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (patched), HTML5 Canvas 2D, Web Audio, optional WebGPU lighting, AI Hub session client 1.4.0; tests on Node and Playwright 1.63.0\",\n    \"artDirection\": \"...\",\n    \"animation\": \"...\",\n    \"audio\": \"...\",\n    \"online\": \"AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 1-4 players; local co-op 1-4 outside sessions\",\n    \"testing\": \"...\",\n    \"research\": \"...\",\n    \"environment\": \"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\",\n    \"model\": \"(your model and reasoning effort)\",\n    \"usage\": \"(estimates if available, else unknown)\"\n  },\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"How many issues did testing catch?\", \"How many were critical?\", \"Most helpful test\", \"Least helpful test\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"Liked most\", \"Stuck or guessed wrong?\", \"Most important thing built\", \"Hardest part\", \"What changed in the engine and why (see ENGINE-CHANGES.md)\", \"One thing to change\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"Liked most\", \"Confusing\", \"Hardest\", \"Would change\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"Gauntletdown\",\n    \"tagline\": \"Four fools. One hungry dungeon.\",\n    \"description\": \"A one-to-four player co-op dungeon crawl inspired by Gauntlet: blast through hordes, smash monster generators and eat before your life force runs out. Play together on one screen, or online in AI Hub sessions.\",\n    \"type\": \"Game\",\n    \"genre\": \"Action\",\n    \"source\": \"Gauntlet\",\n    \"series\": \"Gauntlet\",\n    \"players\": \"1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"Move WASD/left stick · Aim mouse/right stick · Fire click/R2 · Magic right-click/△ · Pause Esc/Options\",\n    \"tags\": [\"top-down\", \"co-op\", \"arcade\", \"fantasy\", \"comedy\", \"retro\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n(The arrays hold your answers, one string per question, in order; the placeholders above show which question goes where.)\n\n## Appendix B: Source Information draft (finish it with your own facts)\n\n- **Date:** from your TASK.md (here 2026-11-03 17:48:04)\n- **Source:** Gauntlet (Atari Games, 1985 arcade)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** arcade co-op dungeon crawler (top-down hack-and-shoot); three-quarter overhead, with top-down and isometric options\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (handoff, with the engine changes in ENGINE-CHANGES.md), Canvas 2D, Web Audio, optional WebGPU lighting, AI Hub session client 1.4.0; Node and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's PS1-era 2D look: procedural HD rigs, textured materials, canvas lighting; three realms (Larder, Ossuary, Furnace Gut) and a banquet hall; new gear, the Shade ghost rig, generators, sprites; generic props from the engine's CC0 asset store\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural rigs and E.MOVES, with new throws, bow draw, eating, starving gait, generator states, ghost cloth, boss choreography\n- **Audio:** nine original chip songs with dynamic layers, synthesized sound effects, gibberish (Babble) voices for the narrator, heroes, monsters and the King; no audio files\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared world, 1–4 players; local co-op 1–4 on its own\n- **Testing:** what you ran (the suites, counts, the harness runs)\n- **Research:** what you looked up (this plan's planner read the engine handoff docs and the AI Hub protocol and harness; Gauntlet facts from the planner's own knowledge, no web research)\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** yours\n- **Usage:** your estimates, or unknown\n\n## Appendix C: Engine and hub facts this plan relies on (verified while planning)\n\n- Engine **v0.85.2** handoff: `my-3d2dge.js` is the full engine with every module; `game/test.mjs` shows the vm loader; `kits/test.mjs` shows the stand-ins (`window`, `addEventListener`, `location`); `tools/check.mjs`, `tools/filmstrip.mjs`, `tools/replay.mjs`, `tools/asset-find.mjs`, `tools/asset-sheet.mjs`, `tools/asset-set.mjs`; `package.json` pins Playwright 1.63.0.\n- `AI_GUIDE.md` has a **Gauntlet recipe** (ADVENTURE slice, `allowJoin({ max: 4 })`, `cam.frame`, one shared FlowField, rigs with `every`) and a **Twin-stick recipe** (`input.padAim`, pointer aim, an `E.Bullets` pool).\n- `E.FlowField` has one target (`update(x, y)`, BFS over 4 neighbours, rebuilt when the target's cell or `map.version` changes): hence E1.\n- `E.Bullets` fields: `x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, r, dmg, life, team, color, core, sprite, pierce, grav, data`; wall hits compare `map.heightAt(x, y) > z` (shots and lobs fly over low walls); no bounce: hence E3.\n- Input presets bind `pause` to `Escape, KeyP, Pad9`; `PAD_GLYPHS` has `playstation` and `xbox`; the `glyphs` setting defaults to `'auto'` in the Controller group: hence E5